<section id="history" class="section-alt">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="reveal mb-12 text-center">
      <span class="badge">Our Story</span>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-navy sm:text-4xl">
        Southeast Asia's First Wind Farm
      </h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-2xl text-stone leading-relaxed">
        From pioneering vision to iconic landmark — how Bangui Bay became the birthplace of
        commercial wind power in Southeast Asia.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Timeline -->
    <div class="reveal mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <div class="relative border-l-2 border-sky-light pl-8 space-y-10">
        <!-- 2005 -->
        <div class="relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[2.15rem] top-1.5 h-4 w-4 rounded-full bg-ocean ring-4 ring-turbine"></div>
          <time class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ocean">2005</time>
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-navy">Commissioning & Phase I</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-stone leading-relaxed">
            The Bangui Wind Farm was commissioned by <strong>NorthWind Power Development
            Corporation</strong>, with 15 Vestas V82 1.65 MW turbines installed along the
            shoreline. This marked the birth of commercial wind energy in Southeast Asia.
            Danish firm Vestas supplied the turbines, and the project was financed partly
            through the Danish International Development Agency (DANIDA).
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 2008 -->
        <div class="relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[2.15rem] top-1.5 h-4 w-4 rounded-full bg-ocean ring-4 ring-turbine"></div>
          <time class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ocean">2008</time>
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-navy">Phase II Expansion</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-stone leading-relaxed">
            Five additional turbines were added, bringing the total to <strong>20 units</strong>
            and expanding the wind farm's capacity to approximately 33 MW. The iconic
            single-row layout stretching across the bay became a powerful visual symbol
            for the Philippines' renewable energy ambitions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 2010s -->
        <div class="relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[2.15rem] top-1.5 h-4 w-4 rounded-full bg-ocean ring-4 ring-turbine"></div>
          <time class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ocean">2010s</time>
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-navy">From Power Plant to Tourist Icon</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-stone leading-relaxed">
            As word spread through travel blogs and social media, the windmills became a
            bucket-list stop on the Ilocos Norte travel circuit. The shoreline viewing area
            naturally evolved into a tourism destination while remaining a fully operational
            power facility. Today, Bangui Windmills power about <strong>40% of Ilocos Norte</strong>'s
            electricity and welcome thousands of visitors annually.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Today -->
        <div class="relative">
          <div class="absolute -left-[2.15rem] top-1.5 h-4 w-4 rounded-full bg-green ring-4 ring-turbine"></div>
          <time class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-green">Today</time>
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-navy">Under ACEN Renewables</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-stone leading-relaxed">
            The NorthWind Bangui facility now operates under <strong>ACEN Corporation</strong>
            (formerly AC Energy), the Ayala Group's energy platform. With a total capacity of
            52 MW across its NorthWind assets, the Bangui site remains a flagship of the
            Philippines' clean energy transition and a model for how renewable infrastructure
            can coexist with thriving tourism.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
